@page "/projects/portfolio"

<ProjectPage Title="This portfolio site"
             Kicker="Live · 2026"
             AccentClass="accent-blue"
             Summary="A serverless portfolio on the Microsoft stack — Blazor WebAssembly in the browser, Azure Functions behind it, deployed automatically and running at zero cost."
             Tech="@(new[] { "C#", ".NET 8", "Blazor WebAssembly", "Azure Functions", "Azure Static Web Apps", "GitHub Actions", "xUnit", "Application Insights" })"
             Links="@(new[] { new ProjectPage.ProjectLink("Source on GitHub", "https://github.com/Ollie1994/portfolio") })">

    <h2>What it is</h2>

    <p>
        The site you're reading. A <strong>Blazor WebAssembly</strong> frontend — C#
        compiled to WebAssembly, running entirely in the browser — with an
        <strong>Azure Functions</strong> API behind it on the isolated worker model.
    </p>

    <p>
        Both are served from a single Azure Static Web Apps resource, so the API sits at
        <code>/api</code> on the same origin: no CORS to configure and no host to hardcode
        across local, preview and production environments. Shared DTOs and validation rules
        live in a third project that both sides compile against, so a change that breaks
        the contract breaks the build rather than production.
    </p>

    <h2>Running at zero cost</h2>

    <p>
        Everything runs on free tiers, and the components were chosen to share one
        property: <strong>they degrade rather than bill.</strong> Static Web Apps has no
        overage billing — exceed the bandwidth allowance and serving pauses instead of
        generating an invoice. Application Insights has a hard daily ingestion cap set, so
        it stops collecting rather than charging.
    </p>

    <p>
        That constraint decided how the contact form works. Sending email looked obvious
        until the cost model: Azure Communication Services bills per message with no free
        allowance, and SendGrid withdrew its free tier in 2025. An anonymous public
        endpoint that sends one paid email per request is an amplifier. Submissions go to
        Application Insights as structured logs instead, where abuse costs availability
        rather than money.
    </p>

    <h2>The API treats every request as hostile</h2>

    <p>
        Blazor WebAssembly runs in the browser, so everything in the client is public, and
        <code>/api/*</code> is anonymous and directly reachable without ever loading the
        site. The endpoint is written accordingly:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>
            Validation rules live in the shared project and run on both sides. The client's
            run is for immediate feedback; <strong>the API's is authoritative</strong>,
            because the client can be bypassed entirely.
        </li>
        <li>
            Request bodies are bounded twice — on the declared <code>Content-Length</code>,
            then again through a limited stream during deserialisation, since the declared
            length comes from the caller.
        </li>
        <li>
            Line breaks are rejected in single-line fields, which removes email header
            injection as a class of bug regardless of how delivery is implemented later.
        </li>
        <li>
            A honeypot field catches naive bots, and their submissions are reported as
            successful — telling a bot it was detected only helps it adapt.
        </li>
        <li>
            Security headers are set at the edge: a Content Security Policy with no
            <code>unsafe-inline</code>, plus <code>nosniff</code>,
            <code>Referrer-Policy</code>, <code>Permissions-Policy</code> and HSTS.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Structured so the tests reach something</h2>

    <p>
        <strong>Function classes are adapters, not logic.</strong> A function reads input,
        delegates to a plain service, and maps the result to a status code. Business logic
        lives in ordinary classes that can be constructed with <code>new</code> in a test —
        no HTTP types, no Functions host, no Azure. Logic written inside a function class
        is effectively untestable, and the test project quietly becomes something that
        can't reach anything worth asserting on.
    </p>

    <h2>How it's built</h2>

    <p>
        Warnings are errors and the .NET analyzers run at
        <code>latest-recommended</code>, so the conventions are enforced by the compiler
        rather than by memory. Every NuGet version lives in one file. CI runs the test
        suite and a formatting check on every push, <code>main</code> requires a passing
        pull request, and each pull request gets its own preview environment. Merging to
        <code>main</code> tests, builds and deploys automatically.
    </p>

</ProjectPage>
